# Enterprise Runbook Standard (ERS)

**Version:** 1.0

**Status:** Approved

**Owner:** Enterprise Runbooks Project

**Last Updated:** 2026-08-06

---

## 1. Purpose

The Enterprise Runbook Standard (ERS) defines the structure, quality requirements, and documentation principles for all operational runbooks within this repository.

Its purpose is to ensure that operational procedures are:

* Consistent
* Repeatable
* Easy to understand
* Auditable
* Maintainable
* Vendor-neutral whenever possible

The standard provides a common documentation framework that enables operations teams to execute technical procedures in a predictable and reliable manner while supporting IT service management best practices.

---

## 2. Scope

This standard applies to all runbooks published within this repository, including but not limited to:

* Infrastructure
* Virtualization
* Storage
* Kubernetes
* Networking
* Security
* Backup & Recovery
* Monitoring
* Disaster Recovery

Every runbook shall follow the structure and requirements defined in this standard unless explicitly documented otherwise.

---

## 3. Objectives

The Enterprise Runbook Standard has the following objectives:

* Establish a consistent documentation structure
* Reduce operational complexity
* Improve service reliability
* Support knowledge transfer
* Facilitate operational excellence
* Align documentation with ITIL 4 principles
* Simplify maintenance and future improvements
* Promote reusable operational procedures

---

## 4. Design Principles

Every runbook should be:

### Standardized

Each document follows the same structure and formatting.

### Practical

Focus on operational execution rather than theoretical concepts.

### Clear

Instructions should be concise, unambiguous, and easy to follow.

### Verifiable

Every completed procedure should include a validation step.

### Recoverable

Whenever applicable, rollback procedures shall be documented.

### Maintainable

Runbooks should be reviewed and updated regularly to remain accurate.

### Secure

No confidential information, credentials, customer data, or production-specific details shall be included in publicly available documentation.

## 5. Document Structure

Every runbook shall follow the standardized structure defined below.

| Section                    |    Required   |
| -------------------------- | :-----------: |
| Document Information       |      Yes      |
| Purpose                    |      Yes      |
| Scope                      |      Yes      |
| Prerequisites              |      Yes      |
| Roles and Responsibilities |      Yes      |
| Risks                      |      Yes      |
| Procedure                  |      Yes      |
| Validation                 |      Yes      |
| Rollback                   | If applicable |
| Escalation                 |      Yes      |
| Documentation              |      Yes      |
| References                 |    Optional   |

Additional sections may be added where necessary but should not replace mandatory sections.

---

## 6. Document Metadata

Every runbook shall include the following metadata.

| Field          | Description                            |
| -------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| Runbook ID     | Unique identifier                      |
| Title          | Runbook title                          |
| Version        | Current document version               |
| Status         | Draft, Review, Approved or Retired     |
| Category       | Infrastructure, Storage, Network, etc. |
| Service        | Related service or platform            |
| Owner          | Responsible person or team             |
| Reviewer       | Assigned reviewer                      |
| Last Updated   | Date of last modification              |
| Next Review    | Planned review date                    |
| Classification | Public, Internal or Confidential       |

---

## 7. Versioning

All runbooks shall follow Semantic Versioning.

| Version | Meaning                                |
| ------- | -------------------------------------- |
| 1.0     | Initial approved release               |
| 1.1     | Minor improvements                     |
| 1.2     | Documentation updates                  |
| 2.0     | Major structural or procedural changes |

Minor corrections such as spelling or formatting updates do not require a new major version.

---

## 8. Document Status

Each runbook shall be assigned one of the following lifecycle states.

| Status     | Description                    |
| ---------- | ------------------------------ |
| Draft      | Work in progress               |
| Review     | Under technical review         |
| Approved   | Official operational procedure |
| Deprecated | Scheduled for replacement      |
| Retired    | No longer valid                |

Only documents with the status **Approved** should be used for operational activities.

---

## 9. Naming Convention

Runbooks shall follow the naming format:

```
RB-XXX-Short-Description.md
```

Examples:

```
RB-001-Proxmox-Cluster-Health-Check.md
RB-002-Ceph-OSD-Replacement.md
RB-003-Kubernetes-Worker-Replacement.md
```

Category folders should use lowercase names.

Examples:

```
runbooks/infrastructure/
runbooks/storage/
runbooks/network/
runbooks/security/
```

---

## 10. Review Policy

Operational documentation must remain accurate and up to date.

The following review intervals are recommended:

| Document Type     | Review Cycle    |
| ----------------- | --------------- |
| Critical Runbooks | Every 6 months  |
| Standard Runbooks | Every 12 months |
| Templates         | Every 12 months |
| Standards         | Every 12 months |

Any significant infrastructure change should trigger an immediate review of affected runbooks.

